ote": "The Roman Thermae of Maximinus, public baths of Bracara Augusta, shrank at the end of the 3rd century; by the 4th century even the theatre fell silent, its stones used for the city walls."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-check-024 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Roman Thermae of Maximinus were public baths [S1]; the baths belonged to Bracara Augusta [S1]; the thermae shrank (their occupied area was substantially reduced) at the end of the 3rd century [S3]; by the 4th century even the theatre was deactivated (fell silent) [S4]; the theatre''s stones were used for the city walls [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-01: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-01-verify-071 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-01/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Roman Thermae of Maximinus are Roman public baths (thermae), the ruins of a monumental building with public baths [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Thermae_of_Maximinus]; The baths belonged to Bracara Augusta (later Braga, Portugal) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Thermae_of_Maximinus]; The baths shrank at the end of the 3rd century [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://imovel.patrimoniocultural.gov.pt/detalhes.php?code=69713]; A theatre was part of the same complex as the baths [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Thermae_of_Maximinus]; By the 4th century even the theatre was out of use (deactivated) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Thermae_of_Maximinus]; The theatre''s stones were used for the city walls [SUPPORTED: https://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bracara_Augusta]"}]'::jsonb),
